<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vidyapati</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1352–<a href="/years/1448">1448</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vidyapati was a poet and scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, best known for his love songs in Maithili on Radha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. He served several kings of the Oiniwar dynasty, notably Shivasimha, and also wrote works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Avahatta. His lyrics influenced devotional poetry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, and he is regarded as the foremost poet of the Maithili language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vidyapati.jpg" alt="Vidyapati" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bijay chaurasia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Maha_Kavi_Kokil_Vidyapati.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Vidyapati was born to a Maithil Brahmin family in the village of Bisapi, now Bisfi, in the present-day Madhubani district of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. His name means master of knowledge. The exact date of his birth is uncertain, because his own works and those of his patrons give conflicting information. His father, Ganapati Thakkura, was said to be a great devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and his great-grandfather Devaditya Thakkura had been minister of war and peace at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harisimhadeva">Harisimhadeva</a>. He was a priest at the court of Raya Ganesvara, the reigning chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirhut">Tirhut</a>. He is recorded as having had two wives, three sons and four daughters.</p><p>Vidyapati worked in the courts of seven kings and two queens of the Oiniwar dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a>. His first commission, from Kirttisimha, produced the Kirtilata, a long praise-poem in verse. Under Devasimha he wrote the prose story collection Bhuparikramana, and he became a close friend of Devasimha's heir Sivasimha. When Sivasimha came to the throne he granted Vidyapati his home village of Bisapi, calling him on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plate the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadeva">Jayadeva</a>.</p><p>In 1406 Sivasimha went missing in a battle with a Muslim army, and Vidyapati and the court took refuge at the court of a king at Rajabanauli, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. Sivasimha's chief queen, Lakhima Devi, then reigned for twelve years. Later Vidyapati served Padmasimha, writing mainly treatises on law and devotional manuals, and by about 1430 he had returned to Bisapi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Vidyapati wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a>, Avahattha and Maithili. Between 1380 and 1406 he composed some five hundred love songs on Radha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> in Maithili, a language that had not been used as a literary medium before him. They continued the tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadeva">Jayadeva</a>'s Gita Govinda, but each song stands alone, and he preferred Radha's point of view to Krishna's. Court singers performed them, and they spread beyond the court. Although he wrote of Krishna, he was not a special devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>: he gave most attention to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>, and he is known for songs of Shiva and Parvati and for the Gosaunik Geet Jai Jai Bhairavi, sung at auspicious ceremonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Bengali Vaishnavas such as Chaitanya and Chandidas adopted his Radha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> songs as hymns, and the literary language Brajabuli developed from Maithili in the sixteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a> imitated it in his Bhanusingha Thakurer Padabali of 1884. His influence has been compared to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and Chaucer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> his love songs are still sung at weddings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vidyapati” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their most famous protege was <b>Vidyapati</b>, whose love songs in Maithili are classics of eastern Indian literature; he served kings such as Shivasimha.</blockquote><a href="/state/oiniwar-dynasty">Oiniwar dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bisfi Vidyapati Dih, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Vidyapati Nagar, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.33,85.88&amp;year=1414" aria-label="Bisfi Vidyapati Dih on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6817 1197 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vidyapati"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7267 1467h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7267 1467h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.33,85.88&amp;year=1414">What was here in 1414? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
